messages.content:
This screen belongs to a clickable mockup of: A tiny bakery website

Show this screen: Menu screen of a small bakery website listing bread, pastries and cakes with prices and add buttons

Screens of this mockup, as a tree:
@home: the first screen of A tiny bakery website
  @menu: Menu screen of a small bakery website listing bread, pastries and cakes with prices and add buttons (this screen)
    @order-checkout: The order checkout screen (not made yet)
      @order-confirmation: The order confirmation screen (not made yet)
  @sourdough-details: Details screen of a sourdough loaf at a bakery website, with description, price and an add to order button (not made yet)
  @croissant-details: Details screen of a butter croissant at a bakery website, with description, price and an add to order button (not made yet)
  @tart-details: Details screen of a lemon tart at a bakery website, with description, price and an add to order button (not made yet)
  @orders: The orders screen (not made yet)

The spec of this mockup: decisions every screen follows, one per line; a row with ? is not decided yet.
product.core-flow = @home → @menu → @order-checkout → @order-confirmation
ui.nav = Tabs: Home @home, Menu @menu, Orders @orders
ui.back ? how Back looks and where it sits
ui.lists ? how bakery items, prices, and add buttons look across lists
ui.forms ? how inputs, validation, and primary buttons are laid out
ui.colors ? which colors mark primary actions, errors, and success
data.prices ? which currency prices use and whether tax is included
data.fulfillment ? whether orders are for pickup, delivery, or both

Tabs of ui.nav: last on every screen, in this markup, one link per tab with the label and @name ui.nav gives it, exactly; the app marks the current tab: <nav data-ui="tabs"><a data-action="@name">Label</a>…</nav>. A ui.nav line for a tab bar starts with Tabs: and names each tab by its label and @name; other navigation is written another way and gets no tab bar.
Back of ui.back: where ui.back says, with its label, in this markup: <a data-ui="back" data-action="back">Label</a>. The tab screens have no Back. A ui.back line starts with the label Back shows, then where it sits.

Design it for a phone screen, 390 px wide.

Design this as a clearly different alternative to the obvious layout: arrange this screen's content another way, not just the wording, and keep whatever the spec decides.

This screen is a step of the main path (product.core-flow): one control on it must open @order-checkout.

